table.research_questions:
1.	Participants are expected to demonstrate evidence of procedural learning in all sessions. This will be indicated by faster and more accurate responses to probable versus improbable trials, particularly during later epochs;
a.	According to the Procedural Deficit Hypothesis, adults with dyslexia are expected to show less evidence of procedural learning than TD adults (Ullman, 2004);
b.	Participants with dyslexia will show slower response times than the TD group (Gabay, Schiff &amp; Vakil, 2012a; 2012b; Henderson &amp; Warmington, 2017) but this will not account for the differences in procedural learning when participants’ average speed is taken into account (e.g. regression slopes); 

2.	Psychometric properties of the SRT task
a.	Both groups will show higher split-half reliability than test-retest reliability, and for the control group these will be in a similar range to that found in previous studies (Oliveira, Hayiou-Thomas &amp; Henderson, 2019, December 10; 2020, September 8).
b.	Reliability in the dyslexic group will be either equivalent to, or lower than, that of the control group (exploratory hypothesis).
c.	Higher test-retest reliability is expected for both groups for later sessions (2 and 3) than between session 1 and 2 (replicating previous findings; Oliveira, Hayiou-Thomas &amp; Henderson, 2019, December 10);

3.	Does procedural learning correlate with languages and literacy measures?
a.	According to Procedural Learning Hypothesis (Ullman, 2004), procedural learning should correlate with language and literacy measures; however, previous studies have found inconsistent results (e.g. Oliveira, Hayiou-Thomas &amp; Henderson, 2020, September 8).;

4.	Does procedural learning correlate with offline attention measures?
a.	Both groups will demonstrate a positive correlation between procedural learning and sustained attention, as measured by the PVT task;
b.	Adults with dyslexia are expected to show higher intra-individual variability in sustained attention than TD adults;
c.	Participants with higher intra-individual variability in sustained attention will show lower test-retest reliability in the SRT task (replicating previous findings; Oliveira, Hayiou-Thomas &amp; Henderson, 2019, December 10).

5.	Do participants with and without dyslexia differ in the amount of explicit awareness of the sequence?
a.	Based on previous studies (e.g. Du &amp; Kelly, 2013; Russeler, Gerthe &amp; Münte, 2006) we expect no significant differences in explicit awareness between groups;

table.measured_variables:
Serial Reaction Time task
The present task is a nonverbal probabilistic task that follows the outline described by West et al. (2018). Each task comprises two second order conditional 12 item sequences. The first two sequences were taken from Shanks, Wilkinson and Channon (2003): probable sequence A – 314324213412; improbable sequence B – 431241321423, the second sequences were taken from Schwaneveldt and Gomez (1998): probable sequence C – 121342314324; improbable sequence D – 123413214243.
For each SRT task, each block starts with the consecutive generation of two random digits (for example: 43), that bigram will then be followed by the location in sequence A (ex. 2) with 90% of probability (probable trial) or the position in sequence B (ex. 1) with 10% chance of occurrence (improbable trial). After each response a new bigram is created which will continuously follow the same principles, with no interstimulus interval.
Response times and accuracy will be measured for each trial. Participants will have to provide a response for each trial to initiate the next trial, regardless of accuracy. Responses will be scored 1 or 0 depending on whether participants press the correct key. Response times below 200 ms will be removed from the analyses and outliers will be identified based on a moving criteria (Van Selst &amp; Jolicoeur, 1994) to replicate the procedures adopted by West et al. (2018).

Language and cognitive tasks
Language and cognitive abilities will also be measured and scored according to standardised guidelines to explore the relationship between procedural learning and individual skills as predicted by the Declarative/Procedural model (Ullman &amp; Pierpoint, 2015). This model proposes that the declarative and procedural memory systems support different components of language, with the procedural memory system underlies the learning and processing of rule or pattern based language components, such as syntax, morphology, and phonology. Furthermore, a few specific tasks were included as they have been found to be impaired in individuals with dyslexia (e.g. nonword repetition). Thus, the following standardised tests and questionnaires were selected to ensure their adequacy for the populations included in the present study.
Adult Reading Questionnaire - a self-report screening tool designed to assess the risk of dyslexia in adults;
Expressive Vocabulary - Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence - Second Edition (WASI-II)
Reading and Spelling - Wechsler Individual Achievement Test, third edition UK (WIAT-III UK);
Nonword repetition - Comprehensive Test of Phonological Processing - Second Edition (CTOPP-2);
Sentence recall - Clinical Evaluation of Language Fundamentals - Fifth Edition (CELF-5 UK);
Nonverbal intelligence will be measured using the Reasoning Matrix II - Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence - II (WASI- II);
Two measures of Attention will be used to better understand the attentional skills of the participants: the Psychomotor Vigilance Task and the Conners' Adult ADHD Rating Scales. Sustained attention will be measured using an online 5-min version of the Psychomotor Vigilance task (Reifman et al., 2018) to explore the relationship between attention and procedural learning.
Generation task: The generation task is used to guarantee that procedural learning in the SRT task remains implicit. Thus, under inclusion conditions, each participant will generate 100 guesses of the underlying sequence learned in the SRT task; whilst the exclusion condition participants will be asked to produce a sequence as different as possible from the training sequence. Each sequence generated by the participants will be coded into the longest consecutive correct guesses and the number of different triplets recalled.
Tasks were designed using Psychopy 3 or Gorilla and the sessions will run online on the Gorilla and Pavlovia platforms.

